#{[17592186553574 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186553406 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553574 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162651" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553574 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553574 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/c7/c7c48912df96585f7af01f3d0c1af904d1ca554555f7c63147fb5e0aa6676884.png)\n\nThe curve ${C}_{1}$ has endpoints $A(1,3)$ and $B(5,3).$ Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=-\\sqrt{{y}^{3}}\\,i+(x-1)^{2}\\,j$ through ${C}_{1}$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector  ${n}_{1},$ as shown above.\n\n*Hint: You're given that $F$ is source-free.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553574 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d222c5e4-f6d8-59cd-b06f-5764984195b7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553574 #edb/ref 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.$\n\n- The flux ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s$ along any closed curve $C\\in D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}\\in D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-\\sqrt{{y}^{3}})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}((x-1)^{2})=0+0=0,\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\nSince $F$ is source-free, we know the flux of $F$ is independent of the path. In particular,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{2}$ is the straight line from $A(1,3)$ to $B(5,3)$ and ${n}_{2}$ is its unit normal, shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/3d/3d64ffb0e4cc313a2857d1c20922c1202619d0bdeb3d97f2808b4995c99f7f9f.png)\n\nNotice that both ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotations of their respective unit tangent vectors.\n\nTo calculate the flux of $F$ across ${C}_{2},$ we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply the formula,\n\n- the curve should be parametrized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector ${n}_{2}$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit normal tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nParametrizing ${C}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =i+3\\,j+t\\cdot [(5\\,i+3\\,j)-(i+3\\,j)] \\\\  & =(4t+1)\\,i+3\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in [0,1].$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =-\\sqrt{{y}^{3}}=-\\sqrt{(3)^{3}}=-\\sqrt{27}, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =(x-1)^{2}=((4t+1)-1)^{2}=16{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t+1)=4, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the flux integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\sqrt{27})\\cdot (0)-(16{t}^{2})\\cdot (4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}-64{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{64{t}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{64}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s=-\\frac{64}{3}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553636 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186553679 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553636 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7efc6d5d-247b-5ec0-8dbf-afc93829600b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553636 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186553499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553641 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "